  "2026-11-27T12:30:35.562Z",codex-agent,"Re-recording branch evidence after adopting the gated sync's item records wholesale (history is a byte-identical prefix-extension of main's; no lines spliced). Reproduction: the retired heading regex measured 1.7s at 800 hostile chars, 13.1s at 1600 (~n^2.8), extrapolating to hours for a 50,000-char line; the retired JSON tokenizer measured linear on every adversarial probe (1MB escaped string 0.03ms, 500k escape pairs 0.2ms, 1MB whitespace 220ms), so alert 1 is a CodeQL false positive on the model, and the tokenizer was still rewritten regex-free so linearity is provable by construction and the alert closes mechanically. Design: headingLines() single-pass per-line parser shared by paragraph() and sectionPath(), byte-identical to the retired regex (curated corpus plus 400 seeded random documents; exact lazy-title and CR-swallow span semantics), and a regex-free JSON scanner with JSON.parse still decoding object keys."
  "2026-11-27T12:30:38.762Z",codex-agent,"Revert proofs: with both changed source files reverted to origin/main, heading-parity.test.ts fails to load (missing headingLines export) and json-spans-linear.test.ts fails exactly once with the hostile-budget test measuring 1831.9ms on the retired tokenizer against its 400ms budget; restored, 7/7 pass. The repo revert harness carries the finer-grained linear heading scan control (retired regex restored by function mutation: exactly one failing test, the 800-space probe at ~1700ms against its 100ms budget, sources restored byte-identically). A JSON-whitespace harness control was withdrawn: the retired loop is also linear, so a timing control straddles the budget under JIT variance (measured 868ms and 1831ms on different runs); the file-level proof stands instead."
  "2026-11-27T12:30:41.838Z",codex-agent,"Gates: typecheck, lint, docstring, duplication 0%, identity and privacy 0 violations; dist rebuilt and dist:check verifies 56 committed artifacts against a pristine build with 168 refused mutations; targeted suites 32/32; coalesced and criteria real-data harnesses pass while the base real-data harness fails 14!=20 identically on pristine origin/main (pre-existing corpus drift on the untouched lines path); changelog regenerated and changelog:check passes; CI rounds: first release-gate run failed only the unchanged 100% branch coverage gate (one uncovered truncated-literal refusal branch, now covered); second round at the coverage-fix head: codeql PASS, test (22) PASS, test (26) PASS 7m15s; all three polynomial-ReDoS alerts stay clear. Work stops here with the item open; the orchestrator verifies and closes."
  "2026-11-27T13:05:41.375Z",codex-agent,"Review follow-up (ancestry bound finding): the class rule is 'a function that receives an explicit bound must not scan past it'. Package audit: sectionPath(text, offset) was the one instance - it parsed every heading line of the whole cited document before breaking at the offset, so anchor() and passageContext() paid a full-document scan even for passages cited at the top of a 16MB source. Fixed by giving headingLines an exclusive end bound (retired-engine-exact for every offset kind, including the NaN and +/-Infinity quirks the unbounded break had - found by the property test) and letting sectionPath scan only lines before the offset. NOT instances of the rule, verified: paragraph() must scan the whole document because heading uniqueness is its contract; hits() must report every occurrence; anchor/passageContext context slices are already length-bounded; adapter maxBytes/timeoutMs are enforced at entry with pinned tests. Revert proof for the bound: with only the bound reverted, the new budget test fails deterministically (bounded region measured 2682.2ms against its 25ms budget, 107x over, no JIT-boundary flakiness) and the property test still passes; restored, 2/2 pass. The property test compares sectionPath against an inline unbounded reference across 300 random documents x 11 offset kinds, so any semantic drift from adding bounds anywhere in the ancestry path fails the suite."
  "2026-11-27T13:39:43.042Z",codex-agent,"Full-gate round: duplication back to 0% (the two property tests now share one randomized document builder; the ancestry oracle was restated independently as a filter/every formulation so no clone pair remains); docstring, lint, typecheck and identity clean; dist rebuilt and dist:check verifies 56 committed artifacts with 168 refused mutations. Local full suite: 148 of 156 tests completed pass, 0 fail, the remaining long-running race tests exceeded the 30-minute local window on this loaded host and are certified by CI as in the previous rounds. Focused suites: 36/36 including the four new bound tests."
  "2026-11-27T14:12:29.975Z",codex-agent,"Review round 2 (short-prefix finding): the class rule is 'do not run a whole-input operation when the target lives in a bounded prefix' - the test-side face of the round-1 production rule. The budget test selected its fragment from the whole sixteen-megabyte document, paying paragraph()'s contract-mandated full-document uniqueness scan (measured 1295-2632ms across runs) for a passage that lives in a twelve-byte prefix. Fixed by selecting the fragment from the short prefix (identical bytes and offsets, so every assertion is unchanged) and budgeting the setup together with the bounded scans; the document build sits outside the timed region after one flake showed allocation cost does not belong in a budget. Package sweep for the class: every other large input in the suites is the assertion target itself (hostile budget cases, bundle size-refusal boundaries, digest fills), not unrelated setup; the production side of the rule was closed in round 1. Revert proof with the final form: reverting only the ancestry bound makes this test fail at 2395.5ms against its 100ms budget (24x over, deterministic); restored, 8/8 pass. Focused suites 36/36; lint, typecheck, docstring, duplication 0%, identity and dist checks all clean."
  "2026-11-27T15:12:31.783Z",codex-agent,"Review round 3: (1) BLOCKING, memory: the class rule is 'a consumer that reduces a sequence to a bounded working set must not first materialize the whole sequence'. The bound fix made sectionPath ask headingLines for every heading before the offset as one array, so a passage cited after four million headings in a supported 16 MB document exhausted a 256 MiB heap where the merge base's lazy matchAll iterator did not. headingLines is now a generator: sectionPath yields-and-reduces with O(1) memory (the round-1 offset bound intact), while paragraph() spreads it once because its uniqueness contract needs the full list exactly as the merge base did. Class sweep: hits() and the paragraph selection are contract-mandated whole-list operations (merge-base parity, not regressions); no other consumer materializes a reducible sequence. New guard: a heap-capped child probe (256 MiB, 4M headings before the passage) resolves the ancestry and exits 0 on the generator and FAILS on the reverted array version (child OOM, deterministic and load-independent) - revert proof run both ways. The harness's linear heading scan control now extracts and mutates the generator form (functionText gained a generator-signature probe) and still yields exactly one failure. (2) NON-BLOCKING, timing: the rule is 'suite timing budgets must tolerate concurrent-runner load'. The 8 MB whitespace case took 811 ms against its 400 ms limit under concurrent verification; the whitespace input is halved and every scan budget widened (whitespace 2000 ms, escaped 1000 ms, digit 800 ms, hostile heading production path 2500 ms) so a 10x loaded slowdown still passes while any super-linear regression still fails by orders of magnitude. These are load-tolerant smoke budgets, not constant-factor revert detectors - the tokenizer's linearity is structural (regex-free single pass) and the recorded old-vs-new measurement stands."
  "2026-11-27T16:22:18.604Z",codex-agent,"Continued round 4 on the existing claim. I will audit parser entry points and add a deterministic guard for the regex-free JSON tokenizer, then verify a function-only revert in a disposable copy."
  "2026-11-27T16:25:47.096Z",codex-agent,"Round 4 rule: a parser replacement needs a negative control that isolates the changed function and checks its implementation invariant independently of runner speed. The old timing checks could pass with the retired tokenizer. In three isolated runs, restoring only merge-base parseSpans produced exactly one named assertion failure with 660 RegExp.exec calls. Restoring the forward scanner passed with zero, while retaining exact lexemes and offsets. All JSON span consumers share selectors.pointer and jsonFragment; the audit found no second tokenizer in command dispatch, rendering, SDK exports, or shipped dist. The existing CommonMark review note describes a genuine difference from legacy ATX behavior, but the requested byte-for-byte legacy acceptance and compatibility with existing citations require preserving the pinned results for attached and closing-only hashes. Issue 87 already tracks the sibling-package audit."
  "2026-11-27T16:34:17.619Z",codex-agent,"Applied the same clock-independent rule to the shared ATX scanner. The disposable revert harness verifies the named assertion for restored heading regex calls and catches an omitted offset through the seeded ancestry property. The linked suite exposed a 165 ms ancestry timing sample on the current scanner. The fixture now materializes its repeated suffix before the timer and retains its four million headings, 100 ms limit and ancestry assertions. The linked suite passed 24/24 after that change and the revert harness passed all 10 isolated controls."
  "2026-11-27T17:09:49.523Z",codex-agent,"Local gate results: focused suites 48/48 including 1024 citations on 64 gitless trackers, final linked parser suite 24/24, linked parser revert harness 10/10 controls, and packed npm and Bun journeys 2/2 with exact changed JSON pointer digests in JSON and TOON. PM_TEST_DEPTH=quick npm test passed 233/233 with at most two workers. Typecheck, lint, docstrings (27 files and 131 declarations), duplication (0%), and dist:check (2/2, 56 artifacts, 168 rejected mutations) passed. Record and criteria revert callers passed their 1 and 6 controls. The coalesced caller has a pre-existing no-op origin/main snapshot replacement, independently reproduced with both helper versions and tracked in pm-evidence-ywae. Strict tracker health passes with extensions disabled. The existing global pm-github version skew is tracked in pm-evidence-local-extension-skew. Exact-head coverage and release validation are delegated to GitHub Actions."
